40	Tasks run in order. Task 1 introduces `tokenize`, which Task 2 (the "does this segment hold a word" test), Task 3 (the word list) and Task 4 (via `wordCount`) all rely on. Task 4 depends on `wordCount` directly. No task edits the tests of an earlier task, and no task rewrites the code of an earlier task: each one only appends a function and widens the export line.
41	
42	### Task 1: `wordCount(text)`
43	
44	**Files:**
45	- Create: `test/wordCount.test.js`
46	- Modify: `src/textstats.js`
47	
48	`wordCount` is the foundation of the module. It also introduces the private `tokenize` helper and the `assertText` argument check that the later tasks reuse, so the rules decided here (what a "word" is, what counts as a joiner) bind every later statistic.
49	
50	**Behaviour.**
51	
52	- A word is a maximal run of Unicode letters or digits (`\p{L}`, `\p{N}`), in any script.
53	- A single straight (`'`) or typographic (`’`) apostrophe, or a single hyphen, joins two runs into one word, but only when a letter or digit sits on both sides. `don't`, `keeper’s` and `well-known` are one word each. `well-` and `dogs'` are the words `well` and `dogs`, with the stray joiner dropped.
54	- Everything else separates words: whitespace, sentence punctuation, em and en dashes, slashes, underscores, and the point in a decimal number or domain name. `3.14` is two words (`3` and `14`) and `example.com` is two (`example` and `com`).
55	- A string with no letters or digits has zero words, however long it is.
56	- A non-string argument throws `TypeError` with a message starting `wordCount:`.
57	
58	- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**
